1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,726
Welcome to Lesson Four of Renaissance Field Lite's Introduction to

2
00:00:03,726 --> 00:00:07,453
Quantum Computing. We are scaling the Bell-pair pattern from two

3
00:00:07,453 --> 00:00:11,856
qubits to three, creating a G H Z state and verifying its measured

4
00:00:11,856 --> 00:00:13,889
correlation with one thousand simulator shots.

5
00:00:14,269 --> 00:00:17,858
Lesson Three used Hadamard and CNOT to correlate two qubits.

6
00:00:17,858 --> 00:00:22,525
Here we keep that structure, add a third qubit, and connect it with

7
00:00:22,525 --> 00:00:26,473
a second CNOT. The circuit grows, but the reasoning stays traceable.

8
00:00:26,853 --> 00:00:28,654
Our objective has three parts.

9
00:00:28,654 --> 00:00:32,978
We will build the three-qubit circuit, predict the only two valid

10
00:00:32,978 --> 00:00:36,941
ideal outcomes, and verify that every measured bit string is fully

11
00:00:36,941 --> 00:00:38,742
matched across all three qubits.

12
00:00:39,122 --> 00:00:42,193
Three measured qubits can produce eight classical strings, from

13
00:00:42,193 --> 00:00:44,581
zero-zero-zero through one-one-one.

14
00:00:44,581 --> 00:00:48,675
The G H Z circuit does not spread probability across all eight.

15
00:00:48,675 --> 00:00:52,428
In the ideal model, it supports only the two fully aligned

16
00:00:52,428 --> 00:00:52,770
endpoints.

17
00:00:53,150 --> 00:00:56,777
The gate order matters. Hadamard first creates equal amplitude on

18
00:00:56,777 --> 00:01:00,766
qubit zero. The first CNOT correlates qubit one with that branch,

19
00:01:00,766 --> 00:01:05,118
and the second CNOT carries the same branch structure from qubit one

20
00:01:05,118 --> 00:01:06,206
to qubit two.

21
00:01:06,586 --> 00:01:10,912
The ideal state is the equal superposition of zero-zero-zero and

22
00:01:10,912 --> 00:01:14,517
one-one-one. Each supported branch has one-half measurement

23
00:01:14,517 --> 00:01:17,761
probability. This formula predicts the output pattern before we

24
00:01:17,761 --> 00:01:18,843
execute any code.

25
00:01:19,223 --> 00:01:21,972
A useful mental model is branch propagation.

26
00:01:21,972 --> 00:01:23,936
Hadamard creates the two branches.

27
00:01:23,936 --> 00:01:27,864
The first controlled X aligns qubit one with qubit zero.

28
00:01:27,864 --> 00:01:32,577
The second aligns qubit two with qubit one, producing a three-bit

29
00:01:32,577 --> 00:01:33,362
shared outcome.

30
00:01:33,742 --> 00:01:37,023
The source imports Quantum Circuit and Statevector Sampler.

31
00:01:37,023 --> 00:01:41,943
The shot count stays fixed at one thousand, and seed sixty-seven

32
00:01:41,943 --> 00:01:45,633
preserves reproducibility. Those constants let us compare the new

33
00:01:45,633 --> 00:01:48,503
circuit structure without changing the measurement contract.

34
00:01:48,882 --> 00:01:50,668
The builder allocates three qubits.

35
00:01:50,668 --> 00:01:55,311
Hadamard acts on qubit zero, then CNOT connects zero to one and one

36
00:01:55,311 --> 00:01:59,597
to two. Measurement is added only after the complete gate chain, so

37
00:01:59,597 --> 00:02:01,740
all three outputs are sampled together.

38
00:02:02,120 --> 00:02:05,882
The main function executes one circuit for one thousand shots and

39
00:02:05,882 --> 00:02:08,960
reads the measured counts. The matched total adds only

40
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:11,355
zero-zero-zero and one-one-one.

41
00:02:11,355 --> 00:02:14,433
Any other string would violate the ideal acceptance condition.

42
00:02:14,813 --> 00:02:18,474
Before running, I predict roughly five hundred zero-zero-zero

43
00:02:18,474 --> 00:02:21,403
outcomes and five hundred one-one-one outcomes.

44
00:02:21,403 --> 00:02:25,065
Finite sampling can shift the split slightly, but every other

45
00:02:25,065 --> 00:02:28,726
three-bit string should remain absent in this ideal simulation.

46
00:02:29,106 --> 00:02:33,103
Now we execute the unchanged lesson source in the local Qiskit

47
00:02:33,103 --> 00:02:36,737
environment. The terminal prints the three-wire circuit, the count

48
00:02:36,737 --> 00:02:39,280
dictionary, and the fully matched acceptance rate.

49
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:42,634
The circuit diagram confirms the intended gate order.

50
00:02:42,634 --> 00:02:46,352
Qubit zero carries Hadamard, the first control reaches qubit one,

51
00:02:46,352 --> 00:02:48,954
and the second control reaches qubit two.

52
00:02:48,954 --> 00:02:52,299
All three measurement blocks appear after the correlation chain.

53
00:02:52,679 --> 00:02:56,713
The measured result is five hundred seven zero-zero-zero outcomes

54
00:02:56,713 --> 00:03:00,013
and four hundred ninety-three one-one-one outcomes.

55
00:03:00,013 --> 00:03:04,046
That is the same seeded split seen in the earlier equal

56
00:03:04,046 --> 00:03:06,613
superposition, now propagated across three measured qubits.

57
00:03:06,993 --> 00:03:08,909
All three acceptance checks pass.

58
00:03:08,909 --> 00:03:12,741
The counts total one thousand, only the two predicted strings

59
00:03:12,741 --> 00:03:16,573
appear, and the fully matched rate is one hundred percent.

60
00:03:16,573 --> 00:03:19,638
The measured evidence matches the circuit-level prediction.

61
00:03:20,018 --> 00:03:23,412
The chart displays all eight computational-basis strings, not just

62
00:03:23,412 --> 00:03:27,145
the two observed values. This makes the structure visible: only the

63
00:03:27,145 --> 00:03:30,878
first and last bars rise, while the six intermediate outcomes remain

64
00:03:30,878 --> 00:03:31,557
at zero.

65
00:03:31,937 --> 00:03:35,731
The individual branch remains probabilistic, but the three bits are

66
00:03:35,731 --> 00:03:39,905
not independent. Once a shot resolves to one branch, all three

67
00:03:39,905 --> 00:03:43,699
measured bits agree. The useful feature is the shared global

68
00:03:43,699 --> 00:03:44,078
pattern.

69
00:03:44,458 --> 00:03:48,301
Adding one qubit doubles the number of possible basis strings from

70
00:03:48,301 --> 00:03:52,843
four to eight. Yet the ideal G H Z circuit preserves a compact

71
00:03:52,843 --> 00:03:56,337
two-outcome pattern. This contrast is why scaling circuit structure

72
00:03:56,337 --> 00:03:56,686
matters.

73
00:03:57,066 --> 00:04:01,133
Verification joins four surfaces: the source code, printed diagram,

74
00:04:01,133 --> 00:04:03,392
measured counts, and acceptance calculation.

75
00:04:03,392 --> 00:04:08,813
All four describe the same H, CNOT, CNOT structure and the same

76
00:04:08,813 --> 00:04:10,169
fully correlated output.

77
00:04:10,549 --> 00:04:13,948
This lesson establishes the expected behavior in an ideal

78
00:04:13,948 --> 00:04:17,347
statevector simulation. It does not measure hardware fidelity or

79
00:04:17,347 --> 00:04:19,613
prove a device-level entanglement benchmark.

80
00:04:19,613 --> 00:04:23,012
Those require separate noise-aware and physical-hardware validation.

81
00:04:23,392 --> 00:04:27,275
On physical hardware, imperfect controlled gates, readout error, and

82
00:04:27,275 --> 00:04:29,432
decoherence can introduce unsupported outcomes.

83
00:04:29,432 --> 00:04:33,746
The ideal result therefore becomes a reference pattern for later

84
00:04:33,746 --> 00:04:37,629
hardware comparison rather than a promise of perfect device

85
00:04:37,629 --> 00:04:38,060
behavior.

86
00:04:38,440 --> 00:04:41,326
Reproducibility depends on more than a screenshot.

87
00:04:41,326 --> 00:04:45,449
We preserve the exact source hash, seed, shot count, terminal

88
00:04:45,449 --> 00:04:49,572
output, chart, captions, and release manifest so the public code

89
00:04:49,572 --> 00:04:52,458
package can recreate the same lesson path.

90
00:04:52,838 --> 00:04:56,625
Lesson Five returns to a single Hadamard circuit and changes only

91
00:04:56,625 --> 00:05:00,413
the number of shots. That lets us isolate finite-sample statistics

92
00:05:00,413 --> 00:05:04,200
and see how a probability estimate steadies as the measurement count

93
00:05:04,200 --> 00:05:04,545
increases.

94
00:05:04,925 --> 00:05:09,192
That completes Lesson Four. The runnable G H Z code and measured

95
00:05:09,192 --> 00:05:12,749
results are linked below. Follow Renaissance Field Lite and Codex

96
00:05:12,749 --> 00:05:16,305
Sixty-Seven for the full twelve-lesson Quantum Foundations series.
